        </p>

        <TopPick />

        <div id="short-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. The Short Answer: Fine to Try the Game, Not to Keep Playing
          </h2>

          <p>
            If a friend invites you to a pickleball session and running shoes are what you own, wear them. Plenty of people try the game that way, and a single relaxed session is unlikely to be a problem. What matters is what happens once you start playing regularly, moving faster and committing to shots near the kitchen line.
          </p>

          <p>
            Running shoes are designed for a very different job. A runner moves forward in a straight line, landing on the heel or midfoot and rolling through to the toe thousands of times. To make that comfortable, many running shoes stack thick, soft foam under the heel, use a rocker shape to roll the foot forward and keep the upper light and flexible.
          </p>

          <p>
            Pickleball asks the foot to do almost the opposite. Players shuffle sideways, stop suddenly, lunge for dinks and pivot to change direction. Those movements load the outside edge of the shoe, and a tall, soft, narrow-based shoe offers less resistance when the foot pushes sideways. That is the main reason court shoes, whether tennis or pickleball-specific, are the standard recommendation.
          </p>

          <p>
            The rest of this guide explains the specific design differences, what they mean for stability and grip, when a running shoe is an acceptable stopgap, and what to buy instead. For specific models, our <a href="/best-pickleball-shoes/">best pickleball shoes</a> guide covers court shoes for indoor and outdoor play.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Running shoes for running, court shoes for court sports.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Running shoes are designed for forward motion. Pickleball is mostly lateral movement, so once you play regularly, a court shoe with a low, stable base and side support is the safer and more effective choice.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="why-different" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Why Running Shoes Are Built Differently
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Stack height and heel drop</h3>

          <p>
            Many running shoes have a high stack, meaning a lot of foam between foot and ground, and a heel that sits noticeably higher than the forefoot. That helps absorb impact over long distances. On a court, though, a taller platform raises the foot and creates more leverage for the ankle to roll when you push sideways. Court shoes generally keep the foot lower and flatter.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Soft, bouncy foam</h3>

          <p>
            Modern running foams are designed to compress and spring back, which feels great on a road. Under a hard lateral push, very soft foam can compress unevenly, letting the foot tilt toward the outside edge. Court shoes typically use firmer midsoles, at least along the edges, to keep the foot level during cuts.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Upper construction</h3>

          <p>
            Running shoe uppers are often light, breathable mesh with minimal structure, because a runner's foot mainly moves forward inside the shoe. Court shoes use firmer uppers, overlays and sidewall reinforcement to hold the foot over the sole during lateral movements. Without that structure, the foot can slide toward the edge of a running shoe when you plant hard.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Outsole shape and pattern</h3>

          <p>
            Running outsoles are often narrower at the midfoot, sometimes rounded at the heel and toe, with tread designed for forward traction. Court outsoles are flatter and wider, usually with herringbone patterns that grip in all directions. Our comparison of <a href="/pickleball-shoes-vs-tennis-shoes/">pickleball shoes and tennis shoes</a> describes the features those two court shoe types share.
          </p>

