        <div id="the-risks" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. The Risks on Court: Stability, Ankles and Comfort
          </h2>

          <p>
            The main concern sports medicine professionals commonly raise about running shoes on court is lateral instability. When a player pushes off sideways in a shoe with a tall, soft heel and little side support, the foot can roll outward over the edge of the sole. Ankle sprains are among the injuries frequently reported in pickleball, and footwear designed for lateral movement is a commonly recommended way to reduce that risk.
          </p>

          <p>
            Footwear is only one factor. Warming up, conditioning, court awareness and not overreaching all matter, and no shoe can prevent every injury. But choosing a shoe built for the movements you are making is one of the simplest steps a player can take, and it costs little more than a pair of running shoes.
          </p>

          <p>
            Comfort is the second issue. Because running shoes let the foot slide inside the upper during side-to-side movement, players sometimes notice blisters on the sides of the feet or toes jamming into the front of the shoe during quick stops. Court shoes hold the foot more securely, which tends to reduce that rubbing.
          </p>

          <p>
            Players with a history of ankle, knee or foot problems should be especially careful, and it is sensible to ask a doctor or physical therapist which footwear suits them. This guide offers general information rather than medical advice, and anyone with ongoing pain should get it checked rather than relying on a change of shoes alone.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="outsole-wear" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Outsole Grip and Wear: The Problem You Notice Later
          </h2>

          <p>
            Running outsoles are designed for forward traction on roads, tracks and trails. On a pickleball court, the grip you need is multidirectional, especially during the quick pivots and lateral shuffles near the kitchen line. A forward-biased tread can feel secure going forward and less secure going sideways.
          </p>

          <p>
            Outdoor pickleball courts are usually coated asphalt or concrete, which is abrasive. Running shoes often use softer, lighter rubber or even exposed foam on parts of the outsole to save weight. On a gritty court, those materials can wear through faster than the tougher rubber used on court shoes, especially under the ball of the foot and the toe.
          </p>

          <p>
            Indoors, the problem flips. Smooth gym floors reward soft, sticky rubber, and some running outsoles do not grip especially well on dusty wood or synthetic floors. Indoor court shoes with gum rubber outsoles usually provide better traction there.
          </p>

          <p>
            Court shoe brands often address durability directly. Some hard court tennis models are sold with outsole durability guarantees, and many outdoor court shoes use thicker rubber in the high-wear zones under the forefoot and around the toe. Those details matter little to a runner but a great deal to a player who drags the back foot during dinks and lunges several times a week.
          </p>

          <p>
            A worn running shoe, with its outsole smoothed and its foam compressed, is the least stable option of all. If running shoes are your only choice, at least use a pair with good tread and a midsole that has not flattened out. Better still, follow the advice in the next sections and switch to a court shoe as soon as you start playing regularly.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="when-ok" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. When Running Shoes Are an Acceptable Stopgap
          </h2>

          <p>
            Running shoes are reasonable for your first few sessions, while you find out whether you enjoy the game. At that stage the pace is usually slower, rallies are shorter and you are less likely to make explosive lateral movements. Buying dedicated court shoes before you know you like the sport is optional.
          </p>

          <p>
            They may also be acceptable for very casual, relaxed games, such as a slow family session in the driveway, where nobody is lunging or sprinting. Even then, a lower, firmer running shoe is better than a highly cushioned, tall-stacked model.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you must use running shoes, choose the most stable pair you own. Look for a lower heel, a wider base, a firmer midsole and an upper that holds your foot securely. Lace them snugly, and consider playing a little more conservatively, avoiding all-out lunges for balls that are clearly out of reach.
          </p>

          <p>
            Pay attention to how your feet and ankles feel after a session in running shoes. If you notice your foot sliding inside the shoe, soreness along the outside of the ankle or blisters on the sides of your toes, treat those as signs that the shoe is not holding you well during lateral movement, and move to a court shoe sooner rather than later.
          </p>

          <p>
            Once you start playing weekly, joining open play or taking lessons, it is time to switch. The pace picks up quickly as you improve, and that is exactly when the lateral stability of a court shoe starts to matter.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="what-instead" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. What to Wear Instead
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Pickleball shoes</h3>

          <p>
            Shoes designed specifically for pickleball combine lateral support, a low, stable platform and outsoles shaped for the quick pivots of the smaller court. Many are a little lighter than traditional tennis shoes. They are the obvious choice for players who play pickleball often and rarely play tennis.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Tennis shoes</h3>

          <p>
            Tennis shoes are excellent for pickleball, with the same lateral support and multidirectional grip. Hard court tennis shoes have durable outsoles suited to outdoor pickleball courts, and the range of models, widths and discounted older versions is very wide.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Indoor court shoes</h3>

          <p>
            For gym floors, volleyball, badminton and indoor-specific court shoes with gum rubber outsoles often grip best. Keep them for indoor use so the soft rubber does not pick up grit or wear down outdoors.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Cross-trainers as a compromise</h3>

          <p>
            Cross-trainers are flatter and more laterally stable than running shoes, so they are a reasonable middle ground for occasional players. They usually lack the durable, court-specific outsole and the level of side support found in dedicated court shoes, so regular players should still move to a proper court shoe. Pair your shoes with a paddle that suits your game, and our <a href="/best-pickleball-paddle/">best pickleball paddles</a> guide covers the options.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="switching" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Making the Switch to Court Shoes
          </h2>

          <p>
            Start with the surface you play on most. For outdoor courts, look for a durable rubber outsole and a herringbone or similar multidirectional pattern. For indoor gym floors, look at indoor court shoes with softer gum rubber. If you play both, two pairs is often the most practical answer.
          </p>

          <p>
            Fit comes next. Try shoes on later in the day with the socks you play in, and test them with side-to-side shuffles and hard stops. Your heel should stay put and your foot should not slide toward the edge of the sole. Players with wide or narrow feet should start with brands that offer their width.
          </p>

          <p>
            Give new court shoes a session or two to settle. Court shoes can feel firmer and lower than running shoes at first, which is the point, but it may take a little getting used to. Most players quickly notice how much more planted they feel during lateral movements.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, keep an eye on wear. Replace court shoes when the outsole tread is worn smooth or the midsole feels flat. Our <a href="/best-pickleball-shoes/">pickleball shoe picks</a> are a good starting point for a first pair, and our explainers on <a href="/indoor-vs-outdoor-pickleball-balls/">indoor vs outdoor balls</a> and <a href="/pickleball-court-dimensions/">court dimensions</a> help explain how court conditions change the way you move.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Keeping running shoes after the first few weeks</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Running shoes are fine for trying the game, but once you play weekly the pace and lateral demands increase. Switching to a court shoe at that point is a simple, inexpensive step that gives your feet and ankles a more stable base.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Choosing the most cushioned running shoe you own</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                If you must use running shoes, a tall, plush model is the worst option on court because it raises the foot and adds leverage for the ankle to roll. Pick your lowest, firmest, most stable pair instead.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Playing in worn-out shoes</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Any shoe with a smooth outsole and flattened midsole loses grip and stability. A tired running shoe is especially unsuited to court play. Check the tread under the ball of the foot and replace shoes once the pattern is gone.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using outdoor shoes on indoor floors</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Outdoor running and court shoes can feel slippery on dusty gym floors. Indoor court shoes with gum rubber outsoles usually grip better, and keeping them indoors only preserves that grip for longer.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Treating shoes as the only safety measure</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Good court shoes help, but they are not a substitute for warming up, conditioning, playing within your limits and getting persistent pain checked. Combine sensible footwear with sensible habits rather than relying on shoes alone.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you wear running shoes for pickleball?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, for occasional or first-time play. They are not ideal for regular play because their tall, soft heels, light uppers and forward-biased outsoles offer less stability during the side-to-side movements pickleball involves. Once you play weekly, switching to a tennis or pickleball court shoe is the safer choice.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Why are running shoes bad for pickleball?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Running shoes are designed for forward motion. Their high stack, soft foam and minimal side support make it easier for the foot to roll outward during lateral cuts, and their outsoles are built for forward traction rather than multidirectional grip. Court shoes are built for the movements pickleball actually involves.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What shoes should I wear for pickleball?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Court shoes: pickleball-specific shoes, tennis shoes, or indoor court shoes for gym floors. All three give a low, stable platform, lateral support and outsoles that grip in every direction. Choose based on your court surface, fit and width, and replace them when the tread wears smooth.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are cross-trainers OK for pickleball?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Cross-trainers are a reasonable compromise for casual players because they are flatter and more stable laterally than running shoes. They usually lack the durable court outsole and full side support of dedicated court shoes, so regular players are better served by tennis or pickleball shoes.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I wear walking shoes for pickleball?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Walking shoes share many of the drawbacks of running shoes, with cushioning and tread designed for forward movement. They are fine for a very gentle session but are not designed for quick lateral cuts. A court shoe is the better choice once you play regularly.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do I need special shoes to start playing pickleball?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Not for your first session or two. Wear the most stable athletic shoes you own and play at a relaxed pace while you learn. If you decide you enjoy the game and start playing weekly, buy a pair of court shoes before your play gets faster and more competitive.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How much do pickleball shoes cost?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Mainstream pickleball and tennis shoes commonly sell for roughly $90 to $160, with discounted older tennis models often available for less. Indoor court shoes cover a similar range. Given how much of the game depends on footwork, a proper court shoe is one of the better-value purchases in the sport.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
